<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harihara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harihara is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deity combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> (Hari) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> (Hara) in one figure, usually shown with one half of the body bearing Shiva's attributes and the other half Vishnu's. Popular in early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the image was especially important in pre-Angkorian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, where the kings of Chenla set up fine Harihara statues in the 6th and 7th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harihara.jpg" alt="Harihara" width="307" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harihara_V%26A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
